  "answer": "Myopia, or near-sightedness, is a condition where a person can see nearby objects clearly but cannot see distant objects clearly. This happens because the image of distant objects is formed in front of the retina instead of on it. To correct myopia, a concave lens is used. This lens diverges the incoming light rays, allowing the image of distant objects to form on the retina. The concave lens is chosen such that it forms an image of an object at infinity at the far point of the person with myopia. This image then acts as an object for the eye lens, which can focus it on the retina. The focal length of the lens is determined using the lens formula, where the object distance is at infinity and the image distance is equal to the far point of the person.",
